WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:08.220
Hi, everyone. My name is Tina Tarconeo, and I am a director on the IBM Z team, and today

2
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:15.060
I want to talk to you about one of my favorite parts of the IBM Z system, and that's its redundancy and availability characteristics.

3
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:17.300
In today's world,

4
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:23.260
workloads are getting stronger and stronger, and we require more and more of them, and we're seeing bigger and

5
00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:30.900
more dense memory available, and so we introduced over ten years ago something called RAME memory,

6
00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:38.500
Redundant Array of Independent Memory. This is one of the key characteristics of what makes the system so available and so reliable.

7
00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:45.780
So as you know, I talked about workloads. Things like in-transaction analytics are becoming more and more prevalent,

8
00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:51.680
and we're increasing the size of memory, right? So in IBM Z14, you can have up to 32 terabytes of memory.

9
00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:58.060
So what is RAME memory? As I said, we introduced it with IBM Z196, and it is

10
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.900
the key characteristics of our availability.

11
00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:08.460
So if you can imagine a processor unit, every processor unit has one memory unit, and within the memory unit is five DIMMs, and

12
00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:12.700
up to one DIMM is set aside for redundant memory.

13
00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:19.260
That means you can lose a channel, and you can lose an entire DIMM and still have complete availability of your memory subsystem.

14
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:27.620
Like I said, this was introduced with IBM Z196 in 2010, and every server that's come out after will include this RAME memory.

15
00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:34.140
Now, what happens if you don't have this? That means in the middle of a transaction, if you have a memory error, data is lost.

16
00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:40.980
That's not good, especially when we have qualities of service and service agreements with our clients, right?

17
00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:47.940
That they expect 100% availability. In fact, that is what the Z in IBM Z stands for, zero downtime.

18
00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:51.020
That includes the server and all of the subsystems within it.

19
00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:57.500
This is just one aspect of how we've built availability and reliability into every subsystem of the IBM mainframe.

20
00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:02.620
That includes the memory, like I talked about, but also the I.O. and the processing units.

21
00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:09.020
So, when I brag about the mainframe and what a great server it is, this is the main reason I like to start with with our clients.
